H.

4
00:00:12,960 --> 00:00:20,080
pylori burrows into the stomach lining and can trigger chronic gastritis, ulcers, and in some cases stomach cancer over time.

5
00:00:20,400 --> 00:00:28,240
Infection rates run highest among older adults, minority populations, and first-generation immigrants from regions where the bacteria is more common.

6
00:00:28,560 --> 00:00:33,920
Here's the part that's changed recently: standard antibiotic treatment is getting harder to rely on.

7
00:00:34,240 --> 00:00:43,440
Resistance to clarithromycin, metronidazole, and levofloxacin — the antibiotics doctors used to reach for first — has climbed above 30% in the US.

8
00:00:43,760 --> 00:00:50,400
That resistance is why gastroenterologists updated their guidelines and now start with bismuth quadruple therapy instead.

9
00:00:50,720 --> 00:00:57,840
Bismuth quadruple therapy combines a stomach-acid reducer, bismuth, and two antibiotics, taken together for fourteen days.

10
00:00:58,160 --> 00:01:03,280
It works around the resistant strains that single- or double-antibiotic regimens increasingly miss.

11
00:01:03,600 --> 00:01:15,760
If you've had a stomach ulcer, unexplained stomach pain that won't quit, or a family history of stomach cancer, that's worth bringing up with your doctor — a simple breath, stool, or blood test can confirm whether H.

12
00:01:16,080 --> 00:01:17,200
pylori is behind it.

13
00:01:17,520 --> 00:01:21,440
Getting tested and treated isn't just about clearing up discomfort now.

14
00:01:21,760 --> 00:01:26,480
It's the one step that actually lowers your long-term risk of ulcers and stomach cancer down the road.
